<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Hardened-runtime entitlements for NeoDrop (the app). Deliberately empty, and that is a finding
     rather than an oversight.

     The app is a single Swift binary that loads no unsigned code, needs no JIT, and is not sandboxed:
     it has to read and write mounted network shares under /Volumes, which the App Sandbox forbids.

     It once declared `com.apple.security.application-groups`, on the theory that a sandboxed Finder
     extension cannot read the app's own Application Support directory and a group container is the
     one place both processes can reach. Measured on macOS 26, that theory does not survive contact
     with an unsandboxed app: writing into `~/Library/Group Containers/…` fails with EPERM — as an
     ad-hoc signature and signed with an Apple Development certificate alike — because group
     containers are reachable only by sandboxed processes. The entitlement made the app *look*
     entitled while the badge index silently never landed.

     What works instead, and needs no entitlement at all, is publishing the index into the extension's
     own container (`FinderStatusIndex.extensionContainerIndexURL`) — provided both bundles are signed
     by one team. Since macOS 14 a container is protected from processes outside the team that owns
     it, so an ad-hoc signature, which has no team at all, is refused with "You don't have permission
     to save the file …" and the badges stay dark. Measured on macOS 26: refused ad-hoc, published the
     moment both bundles carried one Apple Development identity. scripts/verify.sh fails a build whose
     two bundles disagree on the team and notes an ad-hoc one.

     Add anything here only with a reason in a comment, and check scripts/verify.sh still passes: it
     refuses an App Group in this file, because it cannot work in a non-sandboxed app. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
